# Two files from this one (NS-235), both gitignored and never read, printed or edited by agents:
# - .env: the first part below, down to the "Apps" line. Compose and `db:migrate` load it, and it
#   holds the database owner's credentials.
# - .env.app: the "Apps" part. The API and the profile, jobs, funnel and eval commands load only
#   this file. An app refuses to start if DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL, POSTGRES_PASSWORD or a PG*
#   password fallback reaches it, so the owner's credentials stay out of the apps' environment.
#   (The apps still run as your user, next to .env: this is not a file boundary.) DATABASE_URL
#   below must connect as northstar_app, never the owner.
# Replace every "change-me" before `docker compose up` (NS-104). Placeholders only here.

# ===== .env: compose and db:migrate =====

# Postgres 16 + pgvector (app database)
POSTGRES_USER=northstar
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_DB=northstar
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# Node loads these as-is (no ${VAR} expansion), so write each password out, with # / ? @ %
# percent-encoded (%23 %2F %3F %40 %25).
# Used only by `pnpm --filter @northstar/db db:migrate`: the owner, the same user and password as
# POSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_PASSWORD above.
DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgres://northstar:change-me@127.0.0.1:5432/northstar

# Temporal dev server: gRPC frontend and Web UI
TEMPORAL_PORT=7233
TEMPORAL_UI_PORT=8233

# SeaweedFS S3 API (S3-compatible object storage, ADR-0005)
S3_ACCESS_KEY=northstar
S3_SECRET_KEY=change-me
S3_PORT=8333

# Grafana LGTM + OpenTelemetry Collector (ADR-0008)
GRAFANA_PORT=3000
OTLP_GRPC_PORT=4317
OTLP_HTTP_PORT=4318

# ===== Apps: copy everything below into .env.app =====

# The apps connect as northstar_app (NS-212), a role that owns nothing and can only read, insert
# and lock rows on append-only tables. `db:migrate` creates it without a password or login; then,
# once, in psql as the owner: ALTER ROLE northstar_app LOGIN; and \password northstar_app
# (\password keeps the password out of psql's history). Percent-encode the password as above.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://northstar_app:change-me@127.0.0.1:5432/northstar

# Claude API (NS-106). Only `eval --live` / `--record` and the workers use it; tests and replay
# evals never do. Replace change-me with a real key before a live eval: the gateway refuses
# the placeholder.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=change-me

# Google OAuth client for read-only Gmail (NS-113, ADR-0014). In Google Cloud: enable the Gmail
# API, create an OAuth client of type "Desktop app", and add yourself as a test user. Only
# `funnel backfill` uses these; it asks for gmail.readonly and keeps the token in memory only.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=change-me
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me

# Apply Gateway pacing (NS-143, APPLY-007). Optional: unset means 25 a day and 90 s apart on one
# ATS. They can only be lowered: a cap above 25 or a gap under 90 s is refused at startup.
# APPLY_DAILY_CAP=25
# APPLY_MIN_GAP_SECONDS=90

# Worker pools (NS-219, RES-009). Optional: how many tasks each queue's worker runs at once; more
# wait in Temporal's queue. Pipeline: 1 to 16, default 4. Browser: 1 to 4, default 1 (the claim
# ledger still allows one submission per ATS whatever this says).
# WORKER_PIPELINE_CONCURRENCY=4
# WORKER_APPLY_BROWSER_CONCURRENCY=1

# The worker process (NS-225, `pnpm --filter @northstar/worker start`). Optional. Temporal must be
# on this machine (127.0.0.1 or [::1]); unset means 127.0.0.1:7233 and the "default"
# namespace. The apply-browser pool is off unless WORKER_APPLY_BROWSER=on, and then needs the
# installed Chromium's absolute path (nothing is downloaded). Off, nothing can be submitted; on,
# every submission still needs a verified approval and the auto_apply switch.
# TEMPORAL_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:7233
# TEMPORAL_NAMESPACE=default
# WORKER_APPLY_BROWSER=off
# WORKER_CHROMIUM_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium

# Fresh-job alerts (NS-163, SRC-015). Optional. Quiet hours hold alerts until they end (HH:MM-HH:MM,
# may wrap midnight; `off` for none). The zone defaults to this machine's, which is often UTC under
# WSL, so set ALERT_TIME_ZONE there. Tier-1 watched boards
# may alert during quiet hours unless the last setting is false. These are defaults (NS-199):
# `jobs alerts set quiet|zone|tier1 …` stores a value that wins over them, no restart needed.
# ALERT_QUIET_HOURS=22:00-07:00
# ALERT_TIME_ZONE=America/New_York
# ALERT_TIER1_IN_QUIET_HOURS=true

# LLM spend cap (NS-176, RES-009, ADR-0021). Optional US dollars (like 5 or 12.50); unset means no
# cap. A day and a month are UTC. Low-priority routes (the mail backfill) pause at 80 % of a cap,
# normal ones at 100 %, and fresh-job routes keep running up to 125 %.
# LLM_DAILY_CAP_USD=5
# LLM_MONTHLY_CAP_USD=100

# Job embeddings for dedupe suggestions (NS-129, ADR-0033): on or off (unset means off). On, a
# job's title, company and requirements lines (never anything of yours) are sent to OpenAI's
# embeddings endpoint, under the `agent:job-embed` switch, the `openai` breaker and the spend cap
# above. On without a real key refuses to start. Create the key at platform.openai.com.
# Each `jobs` scoring pass then embeds up to 64 jobs whose text changed and suggests duplicates
# (`jobs dupes`); nothing is ever merged.
# JOB_EMBEDDINGS=off
OPENAI_API_KEY=change-me
# How alike two jobs must be (cosine, 0.50-0.99) for a duplicate suggestion. Required while
# JOB_EMBEDDINGS is on, with no default: pick it from the measured set (NS-129 slice 4) first.
# JOB_DUPLICATE_THRESHOLD=0.90

# Recruiter grading in `funnel inbox` (NS-238, #266): on or off (unset means off). On, each run
# reads up to 10 recruiter emails with the recruiter-extract model and grades them in plain code.
# Turn it on only after recording real fixtures for that route (#266).
# RECRUITER_EXTRACT=off
# NS-238 item 2: with RECRUITER_REPLIES=on, `funnel inbox` also files a short reply draft by grade
# for each graded recruiter email you haven't answered (A/B interested, C "share more", D a polite
# no, F nothing) into your Queue. Nothing is sent: each reply waits for your approval.
# RECRUITER_REPLIES=off

# Interview prep in `funnel prep` (NS-241). Your time zone (an IANA name; unset falls back to
# ALERT_TIME_ZONE, and with neither the command stops) and your working hours (HH:MM-HH:MM,
# default 09:00-17:00). Prep blocks are planned inside them.
# PREP_TIME_ZONE=America/New_York
# PREP_WORKING_HOURS=09:00-17:00

# Paul's sign-in for the web app and API (NS-156, ADR-0018). Only the key's SHA-256 goes here,
# never the key. Make a key and its hash in a terminal:
#   KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32); echo "$KEY"; printf %s "$KEY" | sha256sum
# Keep the key in your password manager and paste the 64-character hash below. The API refuses
# to start while this is change-me.
NORTHSTAR_SIGNIN_KEY_SHA256=change-me
# The API on 127.0.0.1, and the web app's origin: open Northstar at this address (the only
# Origin a POST may come from). Its own *.localhost name keeps the session cookie away from the
# other services on 127.0.0.1 (ADR-0018).
NORTHSTAR_API_PORT=8787
NORTHSTAR_WEB_ORIGIN=http://northstar.localhost:3100
# Where the web app finds the API. The web app doesn't read this file: if you change the API's
# port, also export this in the shell before `pnpm --filter @northstar/web build` and `start`.
NORTHSTAR_API_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:8787
# The "Save to Northstar" browser extension's own token (NS-128). Only its SHA-256 goes here, made
# the same way as the sign-in key's; paste the token itself into the extension's options. It opens
# only the extension's endpoints. Left as change-me, the extension is off.
NORTHSTAR_EXTENSION_TOKEN_SHA256=change-me

# Credential vault master key (NS-122, ADR-0010). Path to a file outside this repo holding 32
# random bytes as base64, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32 > ~/.northstar/vault.key` then
# `chmod 600` it. Back the file up somewhere safe: without it, stored tenant passwords can't be
# opened and have to be reset by email. Only the Apply Gateway reads it.
VAULT_MASTER_KEY_FILE=change-me

# Web push to your own browsers (NS-193, ADR-0032): a VAPID key pair you make yourself, no account
# anywhere. Optional: unset (all three commented out) means push is off. Make the pair once in a
# terminal and paste both lines here (the private key only ever goes in .env.app):
#   node -e 'const k=require("crypto").generateKeyPairSync("ec",{namedCurve:"P-256"}).privateKey.export({format:"jwk"});const b=s=>Buffer.from(s,"base64url");console.log("PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="+Buffer.concat([Buffer.from([4]),b(k.x),b(k.y)]).toString("base64url")+"\nPUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="+k.d)'
# PUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT is how a push service can reach you about your pushes: a mailto: or https:
# URL. Making a new pair means every browser has to turn notifications on again. The worker sends
# the pushes (all three settings); the API reads only the public key.
# PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=change-me
# PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=change-me
# PUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:change-me@example.com
